pg| ***** 17 ***** |


UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As I mentioned earlier, 4@S by West was not a good bid because once East bid 3@C, they are in a game forcing auction. |
PG| ***** 18 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Therefore, West should bid 3@S instead which is forcing. 6@D is the best spot, but not sure how you get there. Over 3@S it is not clear what East should bid. 4@D, 4@H (spade slam try with heart control), and 6@S are all possible.|
PG| ***** 19 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The most sophisticated bid available to East, and the one many experts would choose, is 5NT, which is known as ^*Bpick a slam^*n. It basically asks the partnership to cooperatively suggest possible suits for slam. |
PG| ***** 20 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|If West had, for example, 3 card support in either clubs or diamonds, he would bid 6 of that minor. Since he doesn't, he probably would end up bidding 6@S to suggest a very nice spade suit.|
PG| ***** 21 ***** |


