http://tinyurl.com/25tetz89
qx|A2|st||rh||ah|Board 2 IMPs   -   February 21, 2024|pn|hondo717,ashhoki,Eliza42,ladydoc|
md|4SA3HA6D9732CKT762,SKQJ9652HQ972DC95,S8HJT83DJT85CAJ84,ST74HK54DAKQ64CQ3|sv|n|
mb|1D|an|4+ diamonds unless 4=4=3=2|
pg| ***** 23 ***** |


cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Let me discuss East's alert. Many play what East indicated that 1@D promises 4 diamonds unless you are specifically 4-4-3-2. That is a reasonable way to play.|
PG| ***** 24 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The alert is not required, although I always encourage people to alert when in doubt on BBO (since the partner cannot see the alert and does not get any extra information).|
PG| ***** 25 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|However, I will often open 1@C when I am 4-4 in the minors unless my diamonds are much better than my clubs. In this case obviously East has clear 1@D opener.|
PG| ***** 26 ***** |


mb|?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Had East opened 1@H or 1@S I probably pass. On the other hand, I love to bid 2@C over 1@D because it is mildly preemptive Further, even though my suit is not quite strong enough for a 2 level overcall, the tactical advantage makes it a good bid.|
pg| ***** 27 ***** |


mb|2C|
PG| ***** 28 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
mb|2S|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|On this auction in particular, 2@S shows at least 5 spades for sure. With only a 4-card major and enough points to compete, West must make a negative double. 2@H and 2@S always promise a 5-card suit.|
PG| ***** 29 ***** |


mb|3C|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|3S|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|East has an easy 3@S call knowing they have a 8+ card fit.|
PG| ***** 30 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|In fact, even discounting the @CQ (because of the 2@C overcall), East has 15 dummy points  - 12 HCP (not counting the @CQ), 1 quality suit point, 1 length point, and 1 point for the doubleton club. Since West’s 2@S call promised 10+ points, I think it would be reasonable for East to bid 4@S.|
PG| ***** 31 ***** |


mb|P|mb|4S|mb|P|mb|P|mb|P|
pg| ***** 32 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
pc|HJ|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|You definitely try not to lead a suit the opponents have bid. So the @HJ would be a good lead. In this case, although I don't usually like to lead unprotected aces if partner has bid the suit and is likely to have the King, the @CA would be okay here but not in general.
|
PG| ***** 33 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|pc|?|
cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Now West should duck. He sees 4 quick losers and hopes I make a mistake and play the @HA. if he play low, many players in my position, would duck and that would often be correct. |
PG| ***** 34 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Having said that, an expert would take the @HA anyway because the diamonds are formidable and could provide discards and it is quite possible the defense has 4 tricks--1 heart 2 clubs and a spade. |
PG| ***** 35 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As it turns out even if I duck, there are no entries to dummy to play the @DAKQ so it doesn't matter, though it could. For example, if I don't take the @HA and West has a diamond then he gets a discard.|
PG| ***** 36 ***** |


pc|H4|pc|HA|pc|H2|
pg| ***** 37 ***** |


pc|C2|pc|C5|pc|CA|pc|C3|
pg| ***** 38 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|
pc|C4|pc|CQ|pc|CK|pc|C9|
pg| ***** 39 ***** |


pc|S3|pc|S6|pc|S8|pc|ST|
pg| ***** 40 ***** |


pc|S4|pc|SA|pc|S2|pc|C8|
pg| ***** 41 ***** |


mc|9|
pg| ***** 42 ***** |


UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|One of the important skills of a defender is counting your tricks or potential tricks particularly in IMPs, and even in Match Points. You try and count your tricks and figure out a way to take enough tricks to defeat the contract. On this hand, that was fairly straight forward for me.|
PG| ***** 43 ***** |


